---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro'
import Hero from '../../components/Hero.astro'
import SiteIcon from '../../components/primitives/SiteIcon.astro'
import { isParkedLink } from '@/lib/links/parked'

const TRAY_REPO_URL = 'https://github.com/zentala/zntl-tray'

const heroProps = {
  title: 'Case Study',
  subtitle: 'Changing Sedentary Habits Through Behavioral UX Design',
}
---

<Layout
  title="zntlDesk Case Study"
  description="How behavioral UX design, gamification, and automatic detection can change sedentary habits at standing desks. A deep dive into the zntlDesk project."
>
  <Hero {...heroProps} />
  <Hero {...heroProps} />
  <section class="section-full">
    <div class="container-bordered">
      <div class="line-grid line-grid--fill-last">
        <div
          class="padding-responsive-md text-center text-sm text-fg-secondary"
        >
          Behavioral UX
        </div>
        <div
          class="padding-responsive-md text-center text-sm text-fg-secondary"
        >
          IoT / Hardware
        </div>
        <div
          class="padding-responsive-md text-center text-sm text-fg-secondary"
        >
          Rust + React
        </div>
        <div
          class="padding-responsive-md text-center text-sm text-fg-secondary"
        >
          Gamification
        </div>
        <div
          class="padding-responsive-md text-center text-sm text-fg-secondary"
        >
          Open Source
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section-full">
    <div class="container-padded">
      <div class="prose-blog prose mx-auto max-w-4xl">
        <h2>The Problem</h2>

        <p>
          Standing desks are everywhere. They are in offices, home setups, and
          coworking spaces. Yet the data tells a different story: the average
          standing desk owner stands for less than 15% of their workday. The
          desk goes up once in the morning, comes back down, and stays there.
        </p>

        <p>
          This is the <strong>behavior gap</strong> — the distance between owning
          a tool and actually using it. Standing desks are sold as health solutions,
          but they are really just furniture. Without behavioral intervention, nothing
          changes.
        </p>

        <blockquote>
          The problem is not that people lack a standing desk. The problem is
          that nothing tells them when to use it.
        </blockquote>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section-full">
    <div class="container-bordered">
      <div
        class="padding-responsive-md flex h-48 items-center justify-center text-center"
      >
        <span class="text-sm text-fg-subtle"
          >[Image: The Behavior Gap — ownership vs. usage over time]</span
        >
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section-full">
    <div class="container-padded">
      <div class="prose-blog prose mx-auto max-w-4xl">
        <h2>My Approach</h2>

        <p>
          I started from the hypothesis that the problem is not awareness — it
          is friction. People know they should stand more. They just do not act
          on it because nothing in their environment prompts the change at the
          right moment.
        </p>

        <p>The design principles that emerged:</p>

        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Automatic detection, not manual tracking.</strong> A ToF laser
            sensor mounted under the desk detects height changes instantly. The user
            never logs anything.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Coach, not tracker.</strong> The app does not just show data
            — it tells you <em>when</em> to change position, with escalating urgency.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Gamification with real consequences.</strong> Negative scores
            are valid gameplay. If you ignore prompts, your score drops. Comeback
            mechanics make recovery feel rewarding, not punishing.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Progressive escalation.</strong> Gentle visual cues first, then
            stronger nudges, then direct notifications. The system never starts by
            shouting.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Position changes over standing duration.</strong> The goal is
            not to stand for hours — it is to change positions frequently. Two minutes
            of standing counts as a valid position change. The metric that matters
            is transitions per day, not total standing time.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section-full">
    <div class="container-padded">
      <div class="prose-blog prose mx-auto max-w-4xl">
        <h2>The Solution: zntlDesk</h2>

        <p>
          zntlDesk is an open source desktop application built with Tauri (Rust
          backend + React frontend). It pairs with a VL53L1X Time-of-Flight
          sensor on an ESP32-C3 microcontroller to automatically detect desk
          height and user posture.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section-full">
    <div class="container-bordered">
      <div class="line-grid" style="--cols-wide: 2">
        <div class="padding-responsive-md prose-blog prose max-w-none">
          <h5>Hardware</h5>
          <ul class="mt-2">
            <li>VL53L1X ToF sensor (Class 1 eye-safe)</li>
            <li>Seeed XIAO ESP32-C3 MCU</li>
            <li>USB connection (no wireless complexity)</li>
            <li>Under-desk mount, pointing to floor</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="padding-responsive-md prose-blog prose max-w-none">
          <h5>Software</h5>
          <ul class="mt-2">
            <li>Rust backend (Tauri 2) with native WinAPI overlay</li>
            <li>React + TypeScript frontend</li>
            <li>SQLite persistence via tauri-plugin-sql</li>
            <li>Remote phone dashboard via WebSocket</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section-full">
    <div class="container-bordered">
      <div
        class="padding-responsive-md flex h-64 items-center justify-center text-center"
      >
        <span class="text-sm text-fg-subtle"
          >[Screenshot: zntlDesk floating window with KPI dashboard and session
          history]</span
        >
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section-full">
    <div class="container-padded">
      <div class="prose-blog prose mx-auto max-w-4xl">
        <h2>Results</h2>

        <p class="text-sm uppercase tracking-wide text-fg-muted">
          Placeholder data — real metrics collection in progress
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section-full">
    <div class="container-bordered">
      <div class="line-grid">
        <div
          class="padding-responsive-md prose-blog prose max-w-none text-center"
        >
          <p class="mb-1 text-sm text-fg-muted">Standing time / day</p>
          <p class="text-3xl font-light text-fg">
            8% <SiteIcon name="caret-right" size="sm" class="inline text-success" /> 22%
          </p>
        </div>
        <div
          class="padding-responsive-md prose-blog prose max-w-none text-center"
        >
          <p class="mb-1 text-sm text-fg-muted">Position changes / day</p>
          <p class="text-3xl font-light text-fg">
            1.2 <SiteIcon name="caret-right" size="sm" class="inline text-success" /> 4.8
          </p>
        </div>
        <div
          class="padding-responsive-md prose-blog prose max-w-none text-center"
        >
          <p class="mb-1 text-sm text-fg-muted">Longest sitting session</p>
          <p class="text-3xl font-light text-fg">
            3.5h <SiteIcon name="caret-right" size="sm" class="inline text-success" /> 52min
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section-full">
    <div class="container-padded">
      <div class="prose-blog prose mx-auto max-w-4xl">
        <h2>Key UX Innovations</h2>

        <h4>Overlay progress bar</h4>
        <p>
          A 4-pixel bar at the very top of the screen, rendered via native
          WinAPI (not a web layer). It transitions from green through yellow to
          red as a sitting session progresses. It is always visible but never
          intrusive — peripheral vision registers the color shift without
          conscious attention.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section-full">
    <div class="container-bordered">
      <div
        class="padding-responsive-md flex h-24 items-center justify-center text-center"
      >
        <span class="text-sm text-fg-subtle"
          >[Screenshot: Overlay progress bar at different stages — green,
          yellow, red]</span
        >
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section-full">
    <div class="container-padded">
      <div class="prose-blog prose mx-auto max-w-4xl">
        <h4>Coach messaging with comeback mechanics</h4>
        <p>
          The system speaks like a coach, not a dashboard. When you ignore
          alerts, your score drops — but when you finally stand up, the comeback
          message acknowledges the recovery. Negative score is valid gameplay:
          it creates tension that makes the comeback satisfying.
        </p>

        <h4>Widget temperature system</h4>
        <p>
          Visual urgency is communicated through color temperature across all UI
          elements simultaneously — tray icon, overlay bar, popup progress, and
          KPI badges all shift in sync. No text needed. The entire interface
          "heats up" as you sit longer.
        </p>

        <h4>Position changes as the core metric</h4>
        <p>
          Most standing desk trackers optimize for standing duration. zntlDesk
          optimizes for <em>transitions</em>. A 5-minute standing break is worth
          more than a 2-hour standing marathon. This aligns with ergonomic
          research: frequent changes matter more than total time in any single
          position.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section-full">
    <div class="container-padded">
      <div class="prose-blog prose mx-auto max-w-4xl">
        <h2>Technical Depth</h2>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section-full">
    <div class="container-bordered">
      <div class="line-grid" style="--cols-wide: 2">
        <div class="padding-responsive-md prose-blog prose max-w-none">
          <h5>Test coverage</h5>
          <p class="mt-2 text-base text-fg-secondary">
            356 Rust unit tests cover the session state machine, overlay
            rendering logic, break credit calculations, alert firing rules, and
            height calibration. 189 TypeScript tests cover React components,
            hooks, and utilities. Integration tests simulate full sensor-to-UI
            flows.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="padding-responsive-md prose-blog prose max-w-none">
          <h5>Native overlay renderer</h5>
          <p class="mt-2 text-base text-fg-secondary">
            The progress bar is a native WinAPI window — not Electron, not a
            WebView. It uses GDI rendering with
            <code>UpdateLayeredWindow</code> for transparency. This keeps memory
            under 250 MB and avoids the overhead of a second browser process.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="padding-responsive-md prose-blog prose max-w-none">
          <h5>Modular alert state machine</h5>
          <p class="mt-2 text-base text-fg-secondary">
            AlertManager is a pure function: current state in, list of actions
            out. No side effects, no timers, no UI coupling. The tray controller
            executes the actions. This makes the escalation logic fully testable
            and easy to reconfigure.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="padding-responsive-md prose-blog prose max-w-none">
          <h5>Open core model</h5>
          <p class="mt-2 text-base text-fg-secondary">
            The desktop app is open source (MIT/Apache). Cloud sync, AI
            coaching, and smartwatch integration are closed/paid features. This
            lets developers build on the platform while sustaining development
            through SaaS revenue.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section-full">
    <div class="container-padded">
      <div class="prose-blog prose mx-auto max-w-4xl">
        <h2>Links</h2>

        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Product page:</strong>
            <a href="https://desk.labs.zentala.agency"
              >desk.labs.zentala.agency</a
            >
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>GitHub:</strong>
            {
              isParkedLink(TRAY_REPO_URL) ? (
                'github.com/zentala/zntl-tray'
              ) : (
                <a href={TRAY_REPO_URL}>github.com/zentala/zntl-tray</a>
              )
            }
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Research:</strong> Behavioral interventions for sedentary reduction
            — review of the evidence base
            <span class="text-sm text-fg-subtle">[link pending]</span>
          </li>
        </ul>

        <p class="text-center text-base text-fg-muted">
          This is a living project. The app is in active development and the
          results section will be updated with real data as the measurement
          infrastructure matures.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
